>From: dasher@netcom.com (D. Anton Sherwood)
>Subject: Anton attempts synthesis (long)
>
>That's a relief.  Unless I've misunderstood something, Tim's claim about
>perception itself (as distinct from perceptual judgement) is very weak: 
>that sensory data are not without cause.  What are we quarreling about?

I'd put it in a way that may be a bit stronger, without the term "sense
data": perception is usually reliable; when it isn't, the only way we
can know this is by means of other perception or perception-based
cognition such as perceptual judgement, abstraction, concept-formation,
deduction, etc.

>(I was particularly amused to see perception rescued by operationalism.)

And here I haven't been able to figure out what "operationalism" really
mean yet!

>> the failure to identify an assassin camouflaged in bushes you walk past
>> who then jumps and kills you is not a perceptual failure, but a failure
>> of perceptul judgement.  You perceived the assassin; you just didn't
>> judge his identity correctly.
>
>That rather trivializes the evidence of the senses, doesn't it?

No.

>> . . .  The very phrase "pure reason" ought to be banished from
>> conversation until it can take its place as the historical curiosity of
>> an example of a philosophical failure that it is.  I'm no Kantian . . .
>
>Is Tim's objection to that phrase only because of its tainting by Kant?  Is
>it inapplicable for some other reason -- er, on some other grounds -- to the
>language of apodictic axioms?

My objection is to the relegation of necessary truth to the deontological.
I would argue that the irrefutable is in the category of ontological
necessary truth.  Since Kant, "pure reason" has mostly been thought of
as deontological by both its defenders and opponents.  The defense long
since rested, having failed, but its opponents found that they could use
it as an effective strawman against any who tried to defend any necessary
truth at all, even if it be ontological.

>There seems to be some confusion between concepts as abstract pieces of
>information and the mental structures that embody them.  The Starr/Donald
>insistence on concepts as "found, not made" contradicts the definition
>given by Tim, "an integration of particulars based upon their similarities."
>That definition describes the inductive process of making such a mental
>structure.  Can there be a concept of something unique?  (Tim also used
>the word "concept-formation".)

My goal has been to distinguish between designed orders and what Dave
Burns pointed out are "emergent phenomena."  By "made," I intended
"designed," not merely "caused."  An analogy would be the splash one
"makes" when diving into a pool of water.  You don't design your wake
as you move through the medium, although you may try to control it to
some limited extent.  It's an emergent phenomena; in fact, if my
understanding of chaos theory is correct, it can't even be predicted
precisely.

>Maybe I should be asking Starr what
>"exist" means, because he agrees with Donald, but has denied to me that
>points on the Argand plane (complex numbers) exist, and the Argand plane
>seems to me no less real (pardon the pun) than law-space.

I did?  I don't recall doing so.  I don't even recognize what either
"Argand plane" or "complex numbers" mean, although I seem to recall
studying the latter years ago.

>> You [Perry] abuse the term "invention," which entails intentional
>> causation of necessity.  Things can be formed without concepts, but not
>> literally made.  This is a case of confusing the metaphorical and literal
>> uses of words.
>
>Please unpack that phrase "entails intentional causation of necessity" --
>is "of necessity" an adverb on "entails", or a genitive on "causation"?
>If the latter, how is necessity caused?

I meant that invention necessarily entails intentional causation.

>What a thing is does not depend, imho, on how it came to be.

Why not?  I have an argument for why it does, but I'd like to know
what your reasons are before getting into it.

>>  Not all agreements need language to be
>> made, but can be made with gestures and facial expressions alone.  It's
>> highly likely that money originally arose out of such pre-linguistic
>> agreements in at least some societies, if not all.
>
>My guess is that the first barter took place between two people who shared
>a language, that one had to explain the concept to the other.

It occurs to me that it may be possible to test this experimentally: see
if children that haven't acquired language yet can develop barter and/or
money.

>There are known elements of design in many human languages.

And in currency systems.  I argue that these aren't necessary conditions
to the spontaneous orders, and are thus merely accidental.  Occam's
Razor leads me to presume that spontaneous orders have no design in
them in the absence of evidence to the contrary.

