From: ddf2@postoffice.mail.cornell.edu (David Friedman)
Subject: money standards

Anton asks (responding to a chunk of my textbook that I posted)

>Does forex mean anything if there's no legal tender?  Can we expect that the
>hypothetical Japanese investor still needs to convert to instruments issued
>by a locally-known bank?

1. The relevant question is not legal tender but what kind of money the
seller accepts. Barter transactions are legal at present, so if an American
seller wants, say, hondas for his land instead of dollars, the Japanese
investor does not need to get dollars.

2. I think it likely that, even without legal tender laws, there will be
some regional specialization in currency. So the argument goes through,
with "area where people use currency X" or "set of people using currency X"
substituting for "country." It gets more complicated if some people hold
significant amounts of more than one currency.

3. The point of the discussion was to show how the general principle of
comparative advantage works itself out in a particular set of
institutions--the ones we now have. The general argument applies to barter,
private money, ...  . The sense in which trade always balances is more
transparent under barter.

David Friedman

