Well, I think that pi exists, but even if I did not,
rights have a gut level impact that pi does not.  This is
the "kick the stone and if it hurts your foot, the stone is
real" test.

If someone denies the existence of pi, this has no
practical consequences, thus it is reasonable to claim, as
Tim claims, that pi does not exist.

If someone denies the existence of rights, this does have
practical consequences.  Ergo, rights are real by the
kick-the-stone test.

